24. IMPROVE SPACING

Keep the generous whitespace but make sure it does not feel like empty space.

Use whitespace to separate ideas.

Avoid:

Huge blank areas with no visual purpose.

Every large empty area should contain either:

Strong typography
Image
Diagram
Interaction
Metadata
Visual breathing room around an important element
25. ADD VISUAL DEPTH WITHOUT HEAVY EFFECTS

Use:

Thin borders
Slight shadows
Image overlap
Asymmetrical layouts
Accent lines
Small floating labels
Layered typography
Subtle background differences

Do NOT use:

Huge shadows
Glassmorphism everywhere
Neon
Excessive gradients
3D objects
Floating blobs
26. IMPROVE PAGE RHYTHM

The website should alternate between:

Large typography

↓

Interactive element

↓

Visual/project

↓

Editorial text

↓

Interactive section

↓

Project

↓

Personal section

This prevents the page from feeling like a continuous stack of identical sections.

27. ADD SMALL SURPRISES

Introduce subtle moments of personality.

For example:

At the end of the Skills section:

Still learning. Still building.

At the end of Projects:

The next experiment is probably already in progress.

These should be small and understated.

28. MOBILE UX

Do not simply scale down the desktop layout.

For mobile:

Hero becomes vertically stacked
Interactive system becomes a compact vertical diagram
Projects become full-width
Currently Exploring becomes horizontally scrollable
Skills become expandable categories
Navigation becomes a fullscreen menu
Playground becomes swipeable
Remove custom cursor

Maintain generous spacing.

29. ACCESSIBILITY

Maintain:

Strong contrast
Clear typography
Visible focus states
Minimum comfortable touch targets
Logical navigation
Reduced-motion support
No information communicated only through color

The bright theme should remain highly readable.

30. DO NOT CHANGE THESE ELEMENTS

Preserve the strengths of the existing design:

Bright background
Dark typography
Blue accent
Editorial typography
Minimal navbar
Clean buttons
Thin borders
Professional tone
Large headline
Minimal overall aesthetic

Improve the experience, not just the appearance.

31. FINAL UX GOAL

The current website should evolve from:

"A clean portfolio website"

into:

"An interactive personal product that happens to be a portfolio."

The visitor should feel:

This person understands how to design interfaces.

not simply:

This person knows how to code.

Every section should answer one of these questions:

Who is Yatin?

What does he build?

Why did he build it?

How does he think?

How does he work?

What is he learning?

What is he like outside technology?

How can I contact him?

FINAL DESIGN PRINCIPLES

Keep the current visual identity.

Reduce meaningless empty space.

Increase meaningful interaction.

Add real project visuals.

Turn lists into experiences.

Turn projects into stories.

Turn skills into evidence.

Use animation for feedback, not decoration.

Keep the bright professional aesthetic.

Make the website itself demonstrate good UX.